{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["And they embittered him and shot at him — because Potiphar's wife set her eyes upon him, they cast him into the prison house." "And shot at him — a doubling of expression for the arrows in the verse, as in ״יסבו עליו רביו״; ״חציו״. And therefore it has a doubled letter, but it is derived from מריבה, as one says אשר רבו בני ישראל, just as from קם comes קמו, from שב comes שבו — so from הרב comes רב: עם ישראל רבו. And the verse likens the evil tongue of Potiphar's wife to arrows, as it is written: ״חץ שחוט לשונם מרמה דבר״; ״וידרכו את לשונם קשתם שקר״." "And shot at him — derived from רבב. Just as סבו is from סבב, צהלי ורוני is from רנן." "Shot at him — traitrit in the vernacular."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {1 "Rashbam understands the subject as Potiphar's wife; the second clause 'they cast him' likely refers collectively to those responsible for his imprisonment.", 3 "Old French 'traitrir' = to shoot; the lemma is given in the form 'רבו' rather than 'וירבו'."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:47:51.799761464Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}